  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nSynoptic setup: A ridge runs from Nebraska through eastern \nMontana and the high plains of Alberta. A large trough drops south\nalong the Pacific coastline from the Alaska Panhandle to northern\nCalifornia. Between these features Southern to Southwest flow \nresides from central California to western Montana. \n\nToday through Wednesday mid day: Expect mid to high chinook arch clouds\nacross the region as a ridge moves across northeast Montana. This\nshould keep things dry initially. \n\nWednesday late afternoon through Wednesday night: As the ridge\nshifts east SW flow and a mid level disturbance will pass over\nMontana. Afternoon showers over central Montana will expand east\nslowly through the late afternoon and into northeast Montana in \nthe evening. Wind Gusts currently look to be the main threat with\n60+ mph showing HREF Max Wind Gusts. However these gusts appear to\nmoderate out into the evening hours around the time they move \ninto northeast Montana. SPC has a marginal risk for severe\nthunderstorms for areas south of the Missouri River and general \nthunder for areas south. \n\nThursday into Saturday expect dry and above normal conditions.\n\nSunday onward: Chances for rain showers slowly rise through these\nperiods while temperatures drop back down to climatological\nnormal. However model ensemble begins to break apart during this\ntime so individual members are not in agreement with each other\nover details or timing. GAH\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nNo Deviations from NBM were observed. \n\nConfidence is normal to above normal through Friday.\n\nConfidence is normal to below normal from Sunday onward as models\nbegin to fall apart from about Saturday night onward.",
